OVERVIEW:

 This program is a double-linked implementation of an indexed unsorted
 list. Instead of a normal array-based list, it uses nodes that track
 their predecessors and successors, with each node containing its
 respective element.


INCLUDED FILES:

 * ListTester.java - test class with full implementation of scenarios
 * IUDoubleLinkedList.java - double-linked node implementation of an
      indexed unsorted list
 * IndexedUnsortedList.java - interface class which IUDoubleLinkedList
      implements
 * Node.java - allows creation of nodes and links
 * README - documentation


COMPILING AND RUNNING:

 The driver class for testing is already configured to test the
 IUDoubleLinkedList implementation. The program first has to be
 compiled:
 $ javac ListTester.java

 The program is run with the command:
 $ java ListTester

 There are a few arguments when running the program that will change the
 output and/or formatting of the tests:
 * -a | to only print the failed tests
 * -s | to prevent printing the output of toString() calls
 * -m | to prevent printing section summaries

 To clean up the class files from compiling, run the command:
 $ rm *.class


PROGRAM DESIGN AND IMPORTANT CONCEPTS:

 This double-linked list implements the interface of an indexed unsorted
 list; as such, it must remain easily indexable without need for
 organizing the list. This program does exactly that using Nodes instead
 of a direct array to contain the list, and these Nodes carry "previous"
 and "next" links that connect each other to their neighbors. Nodes,
 however, are NOT responsible for the tracking of the beginning (head),
 end (tail), size, or modifications (modCount) of the list.

 Due to the nature of how Nodes are handled in the list, the list does
 not allow easily accessing an index in the list, unlike an array-type
 list would allow. The list is indexable, not indexed. In order to
 handle calls which provide an index, the call's respective method
 iterates through the list to the given index using a for-loop.

 This particular implementation of IndexedUnsortedList allows for the
 use of a private iterator class with extended functionality,
 DLLIterator, which implements the ListIterator interface. This means it
 is only possible to create a DLLIterator object using the methods that
 the list provides. When the iterator is created, it allows for the user
 to start the iterator at a particular index. Otherwise, it is placed at
 the beginning of the list, or head. This iterator is used as a major
 driver for the list's own methods, as most of them create an iterator
 to conduct their function, and are even refactored to have "central"
 functions for adding or deleting Nodes at a specific index when it is
 already known or found.

 Inserting elements into the list has four special cases, which are all
 appropriately handled as such: inserting at the head, inserting at the
 tail, inserting into an empty list, and inserting into a "middle area"
 with no special tracking variables. The way in which the latter-most of
 those cases is handled is very important, because if the Nodes are
 adjusted to handle a new element incorrectly, then some Nodes cannot be
 referred to when updating their "previous" and "next" links. Or, worse
 yet, the new Node's links end up pointing to itself or the wrong
 neighbor, creating a permanent loop that makes the program hang.
 Inserting a Node in the middle requires capturing the next Node's
 predecessor, or the previous Node, before any link can be modified.

 As mentioned previously, a variable exists within this list's objects
 to track the modification count of the list. This is an important
 variable because it allows the iterator class to know when the list was
 modified without the iterator's knowledge. Like any iterator, it makes
 a promise at its creation to return the list's contents truthfully and
 fully. If the list is modified in any manner, it cannot fulfill its
 promise, and throws a ConcurrentModificationException.

 When a DLLIterator object is created, it first analyzes the provided
 index to determine where it should begin its loop. More particularly,
 it finds if the index is at a known point such as head or tail, but
 otherwise finds where the index lands in the list in relation to its
 midpoint (size divided by two), then starts indexing at head or tail
 accordingly.

 A Node-based implementation of an indexed unsorted list with double
 links actually helps in regards to performance and readability, at the
 cost of an extra reference to track another Node object in comparison
 to a single-linked list. Finding, inserting, and deleting elements is
 made simpler, but not quicker (in terms of Big-O performance). Some of
 the biggest gains of double-linked lists are O(1) removal of the tail
 and the iterator methods previous() and remove() not needing trailing
 Node references.

 To my knowledge, there are no remaining improvements to be done to the
 list class, as it is already refactored and rewritten to center around
 functions that take an index for one of its parameters. This is done
 since any operation conducted on the list takes place somewhere in the
 list, at a particular index; for example, there needn't be a separate
 function to handle inserting an element at head when a central function
 could know it takes place at index 0, and this function already handles
 special cases such as this.


TESTING:

 The testing strategy for this program was very scenario-based. The test
 class has been written to implement all 82 change scenarios, leaving
 little room for unknown errors. Each scenario in the test class builds
 a double-linked list in a known state, applies a change or two, and
 verifies the current list's state against what is expected. The list
 and iterator scenarios (such as add(), remove(), etc) all feed the test
 class the same way.

 The edge cases handled in the change scenarios include empty lists,
 one-element lists, two-element lists, and three-element lists. These
 are necessary as the way the list class handles each of them changes
 according to what the list is asked to do, whether it is inserting an
 element at head or tail, making a list completely empty, or inserting
 in the middle of a list. This is also necessary to ensure that a
 DLLIterator object begins and ends where the user intends.

 The provided framework in the test class already handles exceptions
 that the list class can throw. Those namely being:
 * IndexOutOfBoundsException for bad indexes,
 * NoSuchElementException for a lack of a target element, or an empty
      list,
 * IllegalStateException for when the iterator's navigation methods have
      not defined a last-returned Node, and
 * ConcurrentModificationException for when the list was changed without
      the iterator's knowledge.

 The list class does not contain any known bugs, especially any that
 cause failed tests. When the test class is run, all 4,653 tests pass.

 I would not say this program is "idiot-proof"; nothing ever is.
 However, it has been written so idiots have little room to cause
 trouble.


DISCUSSION:

 At one point, I encountered a program hang with a SINGLE scenario; it
 was AB_add1C_ACB, and my debugging of the scenario itself found that
 there wasn't an error in how it was written or implemented. The problem
 was actually trying to insert in the middle of the list in the iterator
 class, and more particularly how the links of the Nodes were ordered to
 change. I tried to refer to the next Node's predecessor, or the
 previous Node, after some links were already changed. I cannot recall
 the exact order of the statements that caused this issue, but the
 simple solution was to first store the previous Node in a reference,
 then write the new Node's links before touching its neighbors. This
 solution also improved the readability of that code segment.

 This project was, by far, simpler than the single-linked project. Being
 allowed to track a previous Node made both performance and readability
 so much better. One improvement I made due to this project was in my
 thinking style. Because of the complex nature of some of the concepts
 in making this list class, I began drawing the pictures and putting my
 ideas onto something tangible and then relating them to each other.
 This made tracking my ideas and learning what doesn't work far easier.